-- Field: division algebra. A commutative ring in which every non-zero element has a
-- multiplicative inverse — for every non-zero `b` there is a `div(a, b)` satisfying the
-- inverse law `mul(div(a, b), b) = a`.
--
-- `Int64` does NOT satisfy Field: truncated integer division discards
-- information (`div(7, 2) * 2 = 6 ≠ 7`). Code that wants integer truncation
-- works over an `EuclideanDomain` instance (`division.anthill`), which is where
-- `Int64`'s `div` lives; code that wants proper division works over a `Field`
-- instance (Float approximately, Rational exactly).
--
-- IT REQUIRED `Numeric` AND THAT WAS A MATHEMATICAL ERROR, not a tidiness one. `Numeric`
-- is `Additive` + `Multiplicative` + `requires PartialOrd[T]`, so through it every field
-- owed an ORDER — and a field has none in general. The two standard counter-examples are
-- not exotic: the complex numbers admit no order compatible with their arithmetic, and a
-- finite field `F_p` admits none either. `algebra.anthill` states this rule for the layer
-- above ("Ring is intentionally Ord-free so non-ordered fields (complex numbers, finite
-- fields) can satisfy it later if needed") and this sort was the one place breaking it.
--
-- MEASURED, and the measurement is a LOAD ERROR rather than an argument. `F_5`, the
-- integers mod 5, backing `add`/`neg`/`zero`/`mul`/`one`/`eq`/`recip`/`div` and providing
-- no order at all, was refused:
--
--   'test.f5.F5' provides 'anthill.prelude.Field', which requires
--   'anthill.prelude.Numeric', but 'test.f5.F5' does not provide 'anthill.prelude.Numeric'
--
-- The carrier is a field by every law this sort states; what it lacked was the comparison
-- surface `Numeric` carries for its CONSUMERS' benefit.
-- `algebra_spec_test::a_field_needs_no_order` is that carrier, and it and
-- `a_field_extension_is_a_vector_space_over_the_base_field` are the two rows that fail
-- when the two `requires` lines below are backed out to `requires Numeric[T]`.
--
-- SO THE REQUIREMENT IS `Ring`, WHICH IS THE STANDARD DEFINITION: a field is a
-- commutative ring with 1 in which every non-zero element has a multiplicative inverse.
-- `Ring` is exactly that base — `provides Additive[T]` and `provides Multiplicative[T]`
-- for the two operations, plus the two laws no single category can state (`mul_comm`,
-- and `distrib`, which reads both operations) — and it is Ord-free by construction,
-- which is the property `Numeric` did not have. `PartialEq` is written out beside it
-- because the guards below spell `eq` and `neq` (`:- eq(a, 0)`, `:- neq(?b, 0)`), and it
-- is the ONE thing the `Numeric` chain was legitimately supplying, via `PartialOrd
-- requires PartialEq[T]`. Dropping `Numeric` without writing it out would have left every
-- guard in this file reading an `eq` no carrier owed.
--
-- THE ASYMMETRY WITH `EuclideanDomain` IS NOW MEANINGFUL rather than accidental. That
-- sibling keeps `requires Numeric[T]` and should: its `constraint mod_nonneg` reads `gt`,
-- so it genuinely consumes the order. The branch that reads an order requires the bundle
-- that carries one; the branch that reads none does not.
--
-- WHAT REQUIRING `Ring` BUYS, and the reason it is worth a cross-namespace `requires`:
-- A FIELD IS NOW A RING, so a field can be the SCALAR of a vector space without saying so
-- twice. `algebra.VectorSpace` declares `requires Ring[F]`, and a FIELD EXTENSION L/K is
-- exactly the statement that L is a K-vector space — so an extension is the first thing
-- that wants a field and a ring to be the same claim. Stated precisely, because the two
-- halves have different weights and only one of them was a hard block:
--
--   * THE HARD BLOCK, measured, was the ORDER — the refusal quoted above, raised for the
--     extension `L` as well as for its base. An extension over a non-ordered base was
--     therefore unwritable at all. Over an ORDERED base it was writable before this
--     change, and that is stated rather than glossed: the defect was never "no extension
--     loads".
--   * THE SECOND HALF is redundancy rather than refusal. `Field` did not imply `Ring`, so
--     every carrier in an extension had to write `provides Ring[…]` by hand beside its
--     `Field` row to reach `VectorSpace` — two rows for one mathematical fact, and the
--     pair can be written inconsistently. That is the same argument `arithmetic.anthill`
--     makes for `provides` over `requires`.
--
-- THE CROSS-NAMESPACE `requires` COSTS A CONSUMER NOTHING. `Ring` lives in
-- `anthill.prelude.algebra`, one namespace over, and a `requires` opens the SPEC IT NAMES
-- and not the module around it (§8.6, WI-20260906-6BX85) — so this line puts `Ring`'s
-- members in reach of a consumer of `Field` and puts nothing else there: not
-- `VectorSpace`, `Ring`'s sibling, and not `anthill.prelude`'s own names through `Field`
-- itself. A consumer may declare its own top-level `Ring`, `List` or `Option` beside a
-- `requires Field[T]` and load clean; `algebra_spec_test::requiring_ring_across_namespaces_costs_a_consumer_nothing`
-- drives both halves.
--
-- IT WAS NOT ALWAYS SO, which is why this paragraph exists at all: until 6BX85 a
-- `requires` re-entered its target's enclosing chain, and 78 of the 79 one-segment
-- `anthill.prelude` names shadowed a consumer's own sort of that name. Naming a sibling
-- ON PURPOSE is still one `import` line — `import anthill.prelude.algebra.{VectorSpace}`
-- — which is also how this file reaches `Ring` (see the imports below).
--
-- WHAT IS STILL MISSING FOR AN EXTENSION, stated so this is not read as closing the case.
-- `VectorSpace[V = L, F = K]` says L is a K-module; it does NOT say K is a SUBFIELD of L.
-- The embedding K -> L, and the coherence `vec_scale(c, x) = mul(inject(c), x)` that makes
-- the scalar action agree with L's own multiplication, cannot be stated — this tower has no
-- notion of a HOMOMORPHISM, and `provides`/`requires` relate a carrier to a SPEC, never a
-- carrier to another carrier. A field extension needs that second relation.
-- WI-20260906-CCT6B owns it.
--
-- ONE MORE THING THE STANDARD DEFINITION SAYS AND THIS ONE CANNOT. The multiplicative
-- group of a field is over `F \ {0}`, not over `F`: that is where `recip` is TOTAL and
-- the inverse law is unconditional. There is no way to name that subset as a carrier, so
-- the statement is made instead by a PARTIAL `recip` carrying a guarded
-- `Error[DivisionByZero]` and by laws guarded on `neq(?b, 0)`. That is a faithful
-- encoding of the same content and is not a defect to repair here; it is the reason this
-- sort requires the multiplicative MONOID (through `Ring`) rather than a group.
--
-- THE LITERALS `0` AND `1` IN THE GUARDS AND LAWS BELOW ARE `Int64`, not `Additive.zero`
-- and `Multiplicative.one`, and with `Ring` now required the honest spellings are in
-- scope. They are left alone here because `Divisible.div` and `EuclideanDomain` write the
-- same `0` and because WI-479's discharge — which drops the `Error[DivisionByZero]` at any
-- call whose divisor is a literal non-zero — reads that literal. Changing it is a change to
-- the whole tower's guard vocabulary, not to this file.
--
-- WI-20260824-VT8CF — `div` IS NOT DECLARED HERE ANY MORE. It is
-- `Divisible.div`, and this sort reaches it by `provides` rather than by a second
-- declaration. Two specs declaring one short name is what `ordered.anthill` refuses for
-- `gt`/`lt` (a carrier providing both would get two `sort_ops` entries and
-- HashMap-iteration order would pick), and here it would also leave the implicit tier
-- with two candidates for the bare `div` that `/` mints — the carrier-dependent name
-- resolution the base spec exists to remove. What is genuinely Field's is `recip` and
-- the inverse law, and those are what remain.
--
-- FIELD IS MATHEMATICALLY A EUCLIDEAN DOMAIN — trivially, with remainder identically 0
-- — and this library deliberately does NOT write `provides EuclideanDomain[T = T]`.
-- That implication is true and useless: the degenerate structure is not what any
-- carrier here computes, and asserting it would owe every field a `mod` answering 0
-- where `Float.fmod` answers the IEEE remainder.
sort anthill.prelude.Field
  import anthill.prelude.{PartialEq, Divisible}
  import anthill.prelude.algebra.{Ring}
  -- WI-20260825-KD9SW: a WRITTEN bare `eq`/`neq` (an effect guard, a law) names the
  -- operation by import now; a minted `=`/`!=` carries its own address and needs nothing.
  import anthill.prelude.PartialEq.{eq, neq}
  -- WI-20260825-KD9SW: a WRITTEN name in a LAW or CONSTRAINT HEAD needs the import.
  import anthill.prelude.Multiplicative.{mul}

  sort T = ?
  requires Ring[T]
  requires PartialEq[T]
  provides Divisible[T = T]

  operation {
    recip(a: T) -> T effects { Error[DivisionByZero] :- eq(a, 0) }         -- `1 / a`; partial: a ≠ 0
  }

  -- Inverse law: div really is the inverse of mul — but only for b ≠ 0.
  -- The guarded-rule syntax (`:- cond`) reads as "this equation holds
  -- when the guard is satisfied." A verifier uses the guard to discharge
  -- the rule's preconditions; a typer/resolver skips the rewrite when the
  -- guard is not (yet) known to hold.
  --
  -- DELIBERATELY UNTAGGED (WI-20260820-8RJK8, which made a `[simp]` guarded equation
  -- fire). The guard says when the equation is TRUE; the tag says whether to ORIENT it,
  -- and these three are not one decision. `div(?a, ?b) = mul(?a, recip(?b))` oriented
  -- left-to-right rewrites every division in the program into a reciprocal multiply —
  -- a normalization choice for the algebra library to make deliberately, not a
  -- consequence of a firing gap closing. The other two are reducing and could be tagged;
  -- whether this file wants a normal form at all is the question, and it is not this
  -- ticket's to answer. They are here for the verifier until it is.
  rule mul(div(?a, ?b), ?b) = ?a :- neq(?b, 0)
  rule div(?a, ?b) = mul(?a, recip(?b)) :- neq(?b, 0)
  rule mul(recip(?a), ?a) = 1 :- neq(?a, 0)
end
